1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,360
School 3A belongs to Weekend Two. Its thinking skills are applied
while learning airway support and medication administration, where a
plausible first explanation can lead to the wrong next action.

2
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:24,960
Reasonable judgment uses relevant evidence, training, patient needs
and context. It is not a promise that every outcome will be good or
that one mental framework proves legal compliance.

3
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:35,064
Your task is to make reasoning visible: what is known, what is
assumed, what could be dangerous and what you will check. A confident
label is not a substitute.

4
00:00:35,064 --> 00:00:47,352
Immediate supportive actions continue while the team checks reasoning.
The purpose is safer action, not analysis so elaborate that a patient
waits without ventilation or hemorrhage control.

5
00:00:47,352 --> 00:01:00,120
The school dual-process model contrasts fast intuitive recognition
with slower deliberate analysis. It is a useful description of
processing styles, not two separate anatomical decision centres.

6
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:12,072
A familiar emergency pattern can trigger rapid helpful action. But a
pattern learned from an incomplete or unusual sample can be
misleading, especially under fatigue or pressure.

7
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:24,360
Deliberate thinking can check a dose, compare alternatives or examine
a mismatch. It is not automatically correct merely because it takes
longer; its inputs and assumptions can still be wrong.

8
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:33,504
Use both as needed. Maintain immediate support, then deliberately test
the part of the plan with the greatest uncertainty or consequence.

9
00:01:33,504 --> 00:01:45,048
Metacognition means monitoring your own thinking. Notice if the urge
to finish a procedure, defend a first impression or avoid looking
uncertain is shaping your decision.

10
00:01:45,048 --> 00:01:54,024
Ask a targeted question: which finding does not fit, what would I
expect if the explanation were correct, and what happens if it is
wrong?

11
00:01:54,024 --> 00:02:04,920
Then obtain a specific cross-check. Ask your partner to verify the
concentration or independently state the most concerning alternative,
rather than asking you agree, right?

12
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:14,472
Reflection must change an observable action. Saying I considered bias
without checking anything new does not show that the assumption was
tested.

13
00:02:14,472 --> 00:02:25,416
Observe means gathering relevant patient and scene information, not
waiting for every possible fact. Distinguish measured findings from a
dispatch interpretation.

14
00:02:25,416 --> 00:02:35,184
Orient means working out what those findings mean in context. Ask what
is dangerous, which alternatives fit and what further information
would change the decision.

15
00:02:35,184 --> 00:02:46,488
Decide identifies the next priority and plan, including delegation or
clinical support. Act means completing that plan with confirmation,
not merely announcing it.

16
00:02:46,488 --> 00:02:56,736
Every intervention and important environmental change produces new
information. Return to observation rather than treating the original
decision as permanent.

17
00:02:56,736 --> 00:03:09,936
The school describes a five-to-ten-second diagnostic timeout. Use it
as a brief team check before a consequential plan or when something no
longer fits, not as an arbitrary delay on every action.

18
00:03:09,936 --> 00:03:21,528
State the immediate concern, recheck critical findings and ask for one
dangerous alternative. Decide whether a new assessment, verification
or resource request is needed.

19
00:03:21,528 --> 00:03:33,744
A partner continues required ventilation or other immediate support.
Cognitive checking and clinical care can occur in parallel; do not
withhold a lifesaving action while finishing a debate.

20
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:46,368
If the answer remains uncertain, acknowledge it and use the
appropriate clinical pathway and support. A timeout is a detection
opportunity, not proof that the resulting decision cannot fail.

21
00:03:46,368 --> 00:03:57,144
Anchoring gives the first story excessive influence. Framing lets the
wording of that story change its apparent meaning, such as a
dismissive label attached to a symptom.

22
00:03:57,144 --> 00:04:10,032
In an original practice call, a handover says difficult patient, but
the observed facts are repeated vomiting, weakness and a new inability
to stand. The label supplies no diagnosis.

23
00:04:10,032 --> 00:04:20,328
Restate the facts without the frame and perform the assessment they
require. Ask when the function changed, what preceded it and what
immediate threats remain.

24
00:04:20,328 --> 00:04:31,440
Do not simply replace one label with another. The safeguard is
independent evidence gathering and reassessment, not a more
sympathetic but equally untested guess.

25
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:43,368
Confirmation bias can make a plausible explanation feel stronger
without new independent support. Repeatedly noticing one fitting clue
is not the same as testing alternatives.

26
00:04:43,368 --> 00:04:55,872
Ask which observation could weaken the leading impression. If symptoms
are attributed to medication, check whether timing, actual dose,
respiratory findings and other causes really fit.

27
00:04:55,872 --> 00:05:08,832
A negative check must be capable of answering the question. A normal
ordinary saturation does not disprove hypoventilation or CO exposure,
so it is the wrong reassuring test for those concerns.

28
00:05:08,832 --> 00:05:18,768
Explain how the result changes your confidence or action. Do not
collect a contradictory finding and then ignore it because it
complicates the original story.

29
00:05:18,768 --> 00:05:28,992
Premature closure stops investigation after finding a plausible
explanation. The explanation can even be true while another important
problem remains.

30
00:05:28,992 --> 00:05:41,280
In this original case, a painful swollen ankle follows a collapse.
Local injury care is reasonable, but the ankle does not explain why
consciousness or balance changed before the fall.

31
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:55,248
Return to the timeline and primary findings, examine the relevant
medical and trauma possibilities and decide which information is time
critical. A complete assessment need not mean delaying transport for
every detail.

32
00:05:55,248 --> 00:06:06,384
Before closing, state what remains unexplained. A documented
uncertainty is safer than a definitive mechanical-fall label that the
findings cannot support.

33
00:06:06,384 --> 00:06:18,912
A recent dramatic case is easy to recall, but that does not make it
the best explanation for the next patient. Compare the actual current
pattern rather than the emotional vividness of memory.

34
00:06:18,912 --> 00:06:32,256
Overconfidence can appear as refusing a reference card or dismissing a
partner check. Experience is valuable when it is calibrated to
evidence and feedback, not when it is used to avoid verification.

35
00:06:32,256 --> 00:06:42,528
Use the actual product label, relevant pathway and measured
observations. Ask a partner for their independent concern before
describing the answer you hope they will confirm.

36
00:06:42,528 --> 00:06:55,032
Review outcomes to learn where confidence was misplaced. Outcome
information must be obtained through authorized clinical or
educational processes, not by accessing unrelated private records.

37
00:06:55,032 --> 00:07:07,368
Omission bias makes inaction feel safer or less blameworthy even when
delay has serious consequences. A decision not to support inadequate
breathing still changes the patient outcome.

38
00:07:07,368 --> 00:07:18,336
Ask whether delay is truly safer or merely more comfortable. Compare
the expected benefit and harm of the authorized action with the harm
of not acting.

39
00:07:18,336 --> 00:07:29,040
Use the actual clinical indication and available support to decide.
Uncertainty about a drug can require clarification while basic patient
support continues.

40
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:41,112
Avoid the opposite mistake of unnecessary treatment simply to look
decisive. The goal is justified care, including appropriate restraint
from an unindicated or harmful action.

41
00:07:41,112 --> 00:07:51,504
Continuation bias keeps the team pursuing a plan after conditions make
it unsafe. Time already invested is not evidence that continuing is
the right choice.

42
00:07:51,504 --> 00:08:02,616
For an original equipment exercise, suppose a patient position begins
to slip during a simulated move. The urge to finish quickly must not
override the agreed stop signal.

43
00:08:02,616 --> 00:08:15,672
Define abort criteria before movement or a complex procedure. Anyone
should be able to state a safety concern; acknowledge it, secure the
patient and reassess personnel, equipment and route.

44
00:08:15,672 --> 00:08:24,744
Restart the OODA loop with the new facts. A revised plan is not
failure; continuing an obsolete plan can be the actual hazard.

45
00:08:24,744 --> 00:08:37,848
Working memory is limited, especially with noise, fatigue and
simultaneous tasks. Group familiar steps into meaningful chunks, but
preserve the purpose and patient response at each stage.

46
00:08:37,848 --> 00:08:50,904
Offload information to a timer, note, approved checklist or partner.
Delegation must specify what to do and what result to report, rather
than vaguely asking someone to handle the monitor.

47
00:08:50,904 --> 00:09:01,800
An external aid is only as useful as its version, readability and
correct use. Reading the wrong concentration perfectly from a
misleading card is still an error.

48
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:12,864
Keep overall ownership: know which critical tasks are complete and
which remain uncertain. Sharing the work is not sharing away
responsibility to notice deterioration.

49
00:09:12,864 --> 00:09:24,000
The school Swiss-cheese model describes several imperfect defense
layers. An error can reach the patient when vulnerabilities align, not
only when one individual lacks care.

50
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:38,232
Its four teaching layers are organizational influences, unsafe
supervision, preconditions for unsafe acts and the active act. These
labels organize investigation; real contributors can cross categories.

51
00:09:38,232 --> 00:09:51,408
For a wrong concentration, examine storage and packaging, availability
of the current reference, workload and supervision, interruption, the
independent check and the actual administration step.

52
00:09:51,408 --> 00:10:02,664
Systems thinking does not eliminate individual accountability. It asks
which changes will make the next error less likely or easier to detect
instead of ending with be more careful.

53
00:10:02,664 --> 00:10:15,072
This original lab case contains no medication order. A syringe has a
familiar volume, and someone says that is what we always use. The
actual concentration has not been checked.

54
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:27,744
Anchoring attaches to the remembered volume; confirmation seeks
reassurance from its appearance; overconfidence may dismiss the
independent check. Several bias patterns can coexist.

55
00:10:27,744 --> 00:10:41,952
Pause the unverified medication step while necessary patient support
continues. Read the actual label, match the intended mass and route,
calculate independently and clarify any discrepancy.

56
00:10:41,952 --> 00:10:55,968
Then examine why the mismatch was possible: storage, packaging,
interruption, reference design and supervision. A successful catch is
a near-miss learning opportunity, not a reason to hide the event.

57
00:10:55,968 --> 00:11:06,288
Knowing a bias name is only a start. Use a structured debrief to
compare what was known at the time, what was assumed, the action and
the observed result.

58
00:11:06,288 --> 00:11:16,512
Avoid hindsight certainty: an adverse outcome does not prove every
earlier decision was unreasonable, and a good outcome does not prove
the process was safe.

59
00:11:16,512 --> 00:11:27,768
Choose a specific improvement, such as an independent label-and-unit
check or a stated trigger for reassessment. Make it observable enough
that a colleague can see whether it happened.

60
00:11:27,768 --> 00:11:39,240
Retest with a different case and seek authorized follow-up feedback. A
supportive learning climate encourages reporting while preserving
responsibility and patient confidentiality.

61
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:50,784
At an airway or medication station, state why the action is indicated
and what finding would change the plan. A memorized procedure without
a clinical reason is incomplete.

62
00:11:50,784 --> 00:12:01,800
Ask a partner to independently verify the high-consequence step, and
explicitly invite a stop for a safety concern. Keep immediate patient
support continuous.

63
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:14,160
After action, examine what changed, what should have changed and
whether anything worsened. Use the response to restart orientation
rather than moving automatically to the next task.

64
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:26,568
Finish with a brief evidence-based debrief using original case
information. This lesson does not access school quizzes, certify a
math gate or replace supervised technical assessment.
